' Canonical order of RSpace match candidates (DR-75, register item I1).
' Phase one sorts candidates by (source hash, store index) and reads only the
' precomputed Produce/Consume hashes. Phase two re-sorts each run of two or
' more equal source hashes by (digest, store index), so only tie-run members
' are serialized and hashed. Play, directive replay and metered native replay
' share the function, so they choose the same candidate.
'
' Source of truth: docs/casper/theory/cost-accounting-decision-records.md (DR-75);
'                  rspace++/src/rspace/candidate_order.rs (canonical_order,
'                  Unmetered); rspace++/src/rspace/replay_rspace/native_candidate/
'                  metered.rs (MeteredOrder).
' Formal model:    formal/rocq/cost_accounted_rho/theories/CandidateSourceOrder.v;
'                  formal/tlaplus/cost_accounted_rho/NativeCandidateOrder.tla.
' Palette:  store read = supply blue · sorting = process lilac · run scan = gate teal
'           digest of a tie-run member = delta amber · single candidate = pool green
'           result = note green · decisions = control amber
' Render:  plantuml -tsvg docs/casper/theory/diagrams/canonical-candidate-order.puml
'          (or: ./render.sh canonical-candidate-order.puml)

@startuml canonical-candidate-order
!include palette.iuml
$ca_base_skin()
skinparam ArrowColor $ca_neutral
skinparam ActivityBorderColor #333333
skinparam ActivityDiamondBackgroundColor $ca_control
skinparam ActivityDiamondBorderColor #333333
title Canonical candidate order: source hash first, digest only for ties (DR-75)

start
:read the candidates of one channel (or channel group)
index each candidate by its store position <latex>i(c) = 0, \ldots, n - 1</latex>; <<$ca_supply>>
if (<latex>n \le 1</latex>?) then (yes)
  :return the candidates as read
  (no sort, no digest); <<$ca_note_green>>
  stop
else (no)
endif
:**phase one**
sort by <latex>(h(c),\, i(c))</latex>
<latex>h(c)</latex> = precomputed Produce.hash or Consume.hash (32 bytes)
no candidate is serialized; <<$ca_process>>
:scan adjacent source hashes
split the sequence into maximal runs of equal <latex>h</latex>; <<$ca_gate>>
repeat
  if (run length <latex>|r| \ge 2</latex>?) then (tie run)
    :digest each member
    <latex>d(c) = \mathrm{Blake2b}_{256}(\mathrm{bincode}(c))</latex>; <<$ca_delta>>
    :**phase two**
    sort the run by <latex>(d(c),\, i(c))</latex>; <<$ca_process>>
  else (single candidate)
    :keep the candidate in place
    (no digest); <<$ca_pool>>
  endif
repeat while (another run?) is (yes)
-> no;
:canonical order <latex>(h(c),\, d(c),\, i(c))</latex>
the same in play, directive replay and metered native replay; <<$ca_note_green>>
stop

note right
  **Why the digest tie-break exists**
  Consume.hash omits peeks and the original pattern order.
  Produce.hash omits the non-determinism metadata.
  An index-only or source-only key would depend on the
  store's insertion order (proved counterexamples).

  **Metering (native replay)**
  Host work is reserved before each allocation,
  comparison, adjacent-hash scan and digest.
end note

legend bottom
  |= colour |= meaning |
  |<$ca_supply>| store read with original indices |
  |<$ca_process>| sort (phase one or phase two) |
  |<$ca_gate>| run detection over adjacent source hashes |
  |<$ca_delta>| digest of a tie-run member (the only serialization) |
  |<$ca_pool>| single candidate: no digest |
  |<$ca_note_green>| result |
  |<$ca_control>| decision |
endlegend

@enduml
